1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,298
Every sentence of modern Japanese weaves

2
00:00:03,298 --> 00:00:05,903
together three different writing systems.

3
00:00:06,203 --> 00:00:11,076
To see why, we have to go back to a
time before Japan had writing at all.

4
00:00:11,076 --> 00:00:15,619
For centuries, the language of
these islands lived only in speech:

5
00:00:15,619 --> 00:00:18,622
in conversation, in song, and in memory.

6
00:00:19,122 --> 00:00:21,301
Writing came from the continent.

7
00:00:21,301 --> 00:00:25,010
Through centuries of contact with
China and the Korean kingdoms,

8
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:26,923
Chinese characters reached Japan,

9
00:00:26,923 --> 00:00:30,245
carried by scribes, scholars
and Buddhist scriptures.

10
00:00:30,545 --> 00:00:33,834
These characters, kanji,
were created for Chinese,

11
00:00:33,834 --> 00:00:35,844
a very different language.

12
00:00:35,844 --> 00:00:40,113
So scribes in Japan adapted them. Some
kanji were used for their meaning.

13
00:00:40,113 --> 00:00:42,936
Others were borrowed purely for their sound.

14
00:00:43,236 --> 00:00:44,901
Written quickly with a brush,

15
00:00:44,901 --> 00:00:48,300
those sound characters became
softer and more flowing.

16
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:50,893
Over generations, the cursive forms were

17
00:00:50,893 --> 00:00:53,671
simplified into a new script: hiragana.

18
00:00:53,971 --> 00:00:58,665
The kanji 安 (an), in flowing
cursive, gradually became あ (a).

19
00:00:58,965 --> 00:01:00,908
Katakana took a different path.

20
00:01:00,908 --> 00:01:04,175
Buddhist monks and scholars,
annotating Chinese texts,

21
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:08,891
needed a quicker shorthand, so they
often wrote just one part of a kanji.

22
00:01:08,891 --> 00:01:12,293
The left side of 伊 (i)
became the katakana イ (i).

23
00:01:12,593 --> 00:01:14,657
Today, all three work together.

24
00:01:14,657 --> 00:01:17,653
Kanji carry meaning.
Hiragana handle grammar.

25
00:01:17,653 --> 00:01:22,031
And katakana mostly write words
borrowed from other languages.

26
00:01:22,531 --> 00:01:26,828
私は日本語を勉強します。 I study Japanese.

27
00:01:26,828 --> 00:01:30,029
Kanji give us "I", "Japanese" and "study".

28
00:01:30,029 --> 00:01:34,926
Hiragana supply the particles
wa and o, and the verb shimasu.

29
00:01:35,226 --> 00:01:38,177
From temple manuscripts
to the screen in your hand,

30
00:01:38,177 --> 00:01:41,667
these scripts still carry
Japanese culture and identity.

31
00:01:41,967 --> 00:01:44,851
Nihongo. One language, three scripts, and

32
00:01:44,851 --> 00:01:48,053
centuries of history in every stroke.

